#
# docker compose reads this file as packaging/.env:   cp packaging/.env.example packaging/.env
# Every value is optional; the comment gives the default.  docs/building.md's Docker section explains the image,
# docs/server.md what each flag and variable does.  packaging/.env is git-ignored and out of the image's build
# context (packaging/Dockerfile.server.dockerignore): the tokens in it stay on the machine.

# What to serve at startup: a Hugging Face name (org/repo:QUANT, downloaded into the flyx-data volume's cache) or
# a file under /models.  Unset starts the server unloaded: pick a model in the dashboard.
#FLYX_MODEL=unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF:UD-Q4_K_XL

# More flyx serve flags on one line (docs/server.md): --sessions, --ctx, --kv, --no-spec, ...
# Split on whitespace, no shell evaluation.  A value with a space in it belongs in the container's arguments
# (`docker run ... flyx:local --name "Inkling dev"`), where quoting survives.
#FLYX_ARGS=--sessions 4

# The port: the container listens on it and docker compose publishes it.  Default 8080.
#FLYX_PORT=8080

# The address the port is published on.  127.0.0.1, the default, keeps the server on your own machine; 0.0.0.0
# publishes it to the network, and that is when a key below stops being optional.
#FLYX_PUBLISH_ADDR=127.0.0.1

# The address the server binds INSIDE the container: 0.0.0.0, and nothing else makes sense with a port mapping.
# FLYX_HOST is here for the rare case of a container on a shared network namespace, where the server should listen on
# one address; it does not control what the host publishes — FLYX_PUBLISH_ADDR does.
#FLYX_HOST=0.0.0.0

# Require Authorization: Bearer <key> on the API.  Set it before publishing the port beyond your own machine, and
# note that through a published port the key is needed for the dashboard too: the request arrives from the bridge's
# gateway address with the machine's name in Host, which is neither localhost nor an address the container trusts
# (docs/building.md).  With a key, open the page once as http://<host>:<port>/#key=KEY; the dashboard remembers it.
#FLYX_API_KEY=

# Which GPU to serve: the number nvidia-smi shows.  One device per process, so one container per card; copy the
# service for the next card.
#GPU_DEVICE=0

# The build: the release baseline image and architectures (80, 86, 89, 90, 100, 120).  One architecture is a fifth
# of the nvcc compiles; keep FLYX_JOBS below the memory you can spare (0: every core).
#CUDA_IMAGE=nvidia/cuda:13.0.2-devel-ubuntu22.04@sha256:1c517d4fb96528c8999e14e6e6b16d7e2ff9ae8d194fd788d60fdc88e693e982
#FLYX_CUDA_ARCHS=89-real
#FLYX_JOBS=6
#FLYX_IMAGE_TAG=local

# Hugging Face: a token for gated repositories, an endpoint for a mirror.
#HF_TOKEN=
#HF_ENDPOINT=

# A folder of your own .gguf files, bound read-only at /models: uncomment its volume line in the compose file too.
#MODELS_DIR=$HOME/models
